BT /F1 10 Tf 54 484.2 Td (time.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 54 446.2 Td (Why Replicate?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 425.0 Td (1. Safety: If one database dies, others have the data) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 411.0 Td (2. Speed: Users read from the nearest copy) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 397.0 Td (3. Load: Spread reading across multiple copies) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 54 359.0 Td (Master-Slave \(Leader-Follower\) Pattern) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 64 333.8 Td (  [Users Write] --> [Master DB] -- copies to --> [Slave 1] <-- [Users Read]) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 64 321.2 Td (                                             --> [Slave 2] <-- [Users Read]) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 64 308.6 Td (                                             --> [Slave 3] <-- [Users Read]) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 292.0 Td (* Master: Handles ALL writes) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 278.0 Td (* Slaves: Handle reads \(copies of master\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 264.0 Td (* One master, many slaves) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 54 226.0 Td (Master-Master Pattern) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 64 200.8 Td (  [Users] --> [Master 1] <-- syncs --> [Master 2] <-- [Users]) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 184.2 Td (* Both can handle reads AND writes) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 170.2 Td (* More complex \(what if both write at the same time?\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 54 132.2 Td (Memory Trick) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 111.0 Td (REPLICA = Redundant Extra Protection Locally In Case of Accidents) Tj ET
endstream
